## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical Azure data pipeline design with beginner-friendly constraints, testing real-world implementation of data isolation, infrastructure as code, and CI/CD practices.

## Key Concepts

- Event Hubs for scalable event ingestion
- Per-tenant data isolation through Azure SQL schemas
- PII filtering and transformation with Azure Functions
- Infrastructure as Code using Bicep
- Continuous integration/deployment with GitHub Actions
- Cross-region disaster recovery via SQL Geo-Replication and RA-GRS
- Cost optimization: autoscaling, partition configuration, retention policies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation of routing logic for per-tenant data
function routeEvent(event){
  const tenantId = event.tenantId;
  // Filter PII and route to appropriate schema
  const filteredEvent = removePII(event);
  await writeToSQL(`tenant_${tenantId}`, filteredEvent);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests real-world trade-offs for privacy, governance, and cost in Azure-native AI at scale, including per-tenant isolation, data lineage, and DR.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant data isolation with per-tenant data partitions
- Azure ML model fine-tuning strategies for tenants
- Data governance using Purview and RBAC
- BYOK with Key Vault for encryption at rest
- DR in paired regions and cost-optimized autoscale

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: resolve per-tenant resources (data path and key)
function tenantResource(tenantId){
  const dataPath = `abfss://data@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/tenants/${tenantId}/chatlogs/`;
  const keyId = `/keys/tenants/${tenantId}/chat-keys`;
  return { dataPath, keyId };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate tenant data isolation under peak load?
- What monitoring would you implement to detect model drift and data leakage?

## Why This Is Asked
Observability is essential for debugging in real-time systems while respecting data residency; this question tests practical Azure visibility patterns.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end telemetry flow in EU
- Per-tenant correlation IDs and distributed tracing
- Diagnostic settings and Log Analytics
- Private Link and EU data residency
- Sampling and retention strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// sample: function emitting traces to App Insights
module.exports = async function (context, eventHubMessages) {
  for (const m of eventHubMessages) {
    const cid = m.properties?.correlationId || context.executionContext?.functionName;
    context.log(`tenant=${m.tenantId} cid=${cid} body=${JSON.stringify(m.body)}`);
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify EU data residency compliance?
- What's your approach for handling multi-region tenant debugging?
- How do you balance observability costs with debugging needs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design privacy-preserving ML pipelines across tenants with governance constraints. It also probes how to coordinate training, model registry, and DR while controlling cost.

## Key Concepts
- Federated learning across tenants
- Azure ML orchestration and Delta Lake
- Purview RBAC BYOK governance
- Drift monitoring and auditability
- DR and cost optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for federated training loop
function train_round(tenant_config, local_data_uri, model, aggregator_uri){
  const local_model = train(model, load(local_data_uri));
  const update = compute_update(local_model, model);
  send_update_to_server(aggregator_uri, update);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect and respond to drift across tenants?
- How would you handle non-IID data and stragglers in the federation?
